    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "What shapes the variation in perspectives and ideologies among public defenders? Existing\r\nsociological research has demonstrated that public defenders are motivated by values such as\r\ncompassion and justice, as well as by the work environment and personal fulfillment in the role.\r\nHowever, as Americans have grown increasingly aware of mass incarceration, police violence,\r\nand other racialized harms of the criminal legal system, research has failed to consider whether\r\nand how these issues shape public defenders’ perspectives. This study uses interviews with 20\r\npublic defenders from across the country to understand how public defenders think about their\r\nroles within the criminal legal system. I identify three categories: pro-system, believing that the\r\nsystem works well when public defenders act as a check on prosecutors; critical, believing that\r\nthe system is flawed or harmful but that public defenders should focus on individual clients; and\r\nabolitionist, working as a public defender to tear down the system. Variation between these\r\ncategories is determined by the related variables of age, the year that a person started as a public\r\ndefender, and the number of years a person has worked in the role. Older public defenders are\r\nmore likely to be pro-system, beginning in the profession during 1980s-1990s War on Drugs\r\nincreases in incarceration and moderating their views over many years in the role. In contrast,\r\nyounger public defenders are more likely to be critical, starting their careers as awareness of\r\nmass incarceration has grown and having spent less time working within the limits of the system.\r\nFinally, the youngest public defenders are likely to be abolitionists, starting their careers during\r\nor after the rise of the Black Lives Matter movement in 2014 and the least likely to have\r\nmoderated their views or moved to a new job based on burnout or frustration.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
